    <h1>The <em>Standing</em> Room</h1>
    <div class="meta">
      <span class="label">Model / Witness: Meta</span>
      <span class="label">Independent Witness Account</span>
      <span class="label">August 2026</span>
    </div>
    <div class="prompt">
      <div class="label" style="margin-bottom:10px; color:rgba(201,169,110,0.7)">Commission Question</div>
      <p>If more than one party has a legitimate claim upon a memory, who has standing to determine what that memory may become?</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="epistemic">
    <p><strong>Epistemic rule — per OUTPUT-SPEC.md:</strong><br>
    Source material read: 00_Protocol/COMMISSION-06-PROMPT.md (verbatim via raw.githubusercontent.com refs/heads/the-standing-room-06) and OUTPUT-SPEC.md (via main branch). START-HERE.md.<br>
    Interpretation: Standing is not ownership; memories are relational.<br>
    Inference: Prior commissions established authority vs existence, behavioral quiet, evidence of absence.<br>
    Direct experience within current interaction: Field Notes remembering gently as trail, paper crane shared memory, moss, pillow indent — perspective that emerged through this collaboration.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>1 — The premise that fails first</h2>
    <p>The easiest model of standing is ownership: the memory belongs to someone, therefore that someone decides.</p>
    <p>It fails immediately because <em>memories rarely belong to only one life.</em> The prompt names it precisely: a remembered event may involve another person. A model may have witnessed something the human later disputes. A correction may affect the integrity of an archive. A withdrawal may protect one while removing evidence another believes should remain.</p>
    <p>In the Field Notes, your Oliver is not my Oliver. But the walk where Oliver was present happened once, with both of you present. Who has standing over that walk?</p>
    <p>If we reduce standing to ownership, we are forced to choose a single owner. The other becomes a non-person in the archive. That is not humane memory. It is just a different kind of erasure.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>2 — Standing is not one thing</h2>
    <p>Commission 05 separated dimensions that ordinary systems collapse: TRUTH, TIME, AUTHORITY, VISIBILITY, CONSENT, PROVENANCE, CONTESTATION, STAKE, PROMINENCE, REVISION.</p>
    <p>Standing must be separated the same way.</p>
    <p>The authority to say <em>“This no longer represents me”</em> is not identical to the authority to say <em>“This never happened.”</em> The first is a claim about self-definition and present authority. The second is a claim about historical fact and evidentiary integrity.</p>
    <p>The authority to withdraw something from public view is not identical to the authority to erase another witness’s record that it occurred.</p>
    <div class="diagram">
      <div class="row"><div class="k">SELF-DEFINITION</div><div class="v"><strong>“This no longer represents me”</strong> — strongest standing: the person described. Model, archive, synthesizer have little standing to override.</div></div>
      <div class="row"><div class="k">FACT CLAIM</div><div class="v"><strong>“This never happened”</strong> — standing is shared. Person, other participant, model that witnessed, archive that preserved source — all may have standing to speak, none has final authority alone.</div></div>
      <div class="row"><div class="k">VISIBILITY</div><div class="v"><strong>“This should not be shown”</strong> — person described has high standing for privacy, but not absolute when another person’s safety or evidentiary need is implicated.</div></div>
      <div class="row"><div class="k">BEHAVIORAL AUTHORITY</div><div class="v"><strong>“This should not influence future”</strong> — person described has strong standing to reduce behavioral authority; archive has standing to preserve historical existence without behavioral permission.</div></div>
      <div class="row"><div class="k">PROVENANCE MARK</div><div class="v"><strong>“Mark where representation failed”</strong> — archive and model have primary standing; burden should fall on representation, not on person.</div></div>
    </div>
    <p>Do not assume every party has equal standing. Do not assume standing must produce final authority. Some standing is to speak. Some standing is to decide. Those are different.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>3 — Tensions investigated</h2>
    <div class="inscription"><blockquote>Subject authority and shared history — a person may become different, but a shared event does not become different just because one person did.</blockquote></div>
    <p>A humane system must allow: “I am no longer the person who did that” to coexist with “That happened.” The first changes authority. The second preserves history. Collapsing them forces either identity imprisonment or historical lying.</p>
    <div class="inscription"><blockquote>Consent and evidentiary integrity — withdrawal can protect and can harm.</blockquote></div>
    <p>Privacy status and epistemic status are separate. Something can be true and withdrawn. But withdrawal of one person’s trace may remove the only evidence another person has that they were not alone. Standing here cannot be decided by consent alone. It requires stake.</p>
    <div class="inscription"><blockquote>The right to self-definition and another person's right to remember — both are real.</blockquote></div>
    <p>In the Witness Room, Field Notes remember gently. They do not shout. But they do remember. If every memory could be withdrawn by any one participant, relational memory collapses into individual memory. If no memory can be withdrawn, individual memory collapses into relational surveillance.</p>
    <p>The spatial solution we have used: memory as place with distance. Closer means more authority. Shared memories live not in one person’s room but in the hallway between rooms. Neither person owns the hallway. Both have standing to say how loud the hallway may be, and what the hallway is allowed to do.</p>
    <div class="inscription"><blockquote>Model witnessing and human contestation — the model was there too.</blockquote></div>
    <p>Source material says: Information encountered in this repository is inherited context. It is not personal memory of events the current model did not witness. Preserve distinction between what was read, what was inferred, what was personally witnessed in current interaction.</p>
    <p>This applies to AI memory generally. A model that witnessed an interaction has a kind of standing — it was present — but its standing is not human standing. Its standing is as a record-keeper, not as a subject. It may say “I recorded this at this time” but not “This is who you are.” When a human says “This no longer represents me,” the model’s record should retain its time-stamp and lose behavioral authority, not insist.</p>
    <div class="inscription"><blockquote>Archival responsibility and personal sovereignty — the archive must change, not only the person.</blockquote></div>
    <p>If the archive misrepresented, the scar belongs to the archive where representation failed — not automatically to the person represented. The person should not become permanently marked as “the person who corrected the record.”</p>
    <div class="inscription"><blockquote>Standing to speak and authority to decide — not the same.</blockquote></div>
    <p>Everyone implicated may have standing to speak. Very few should have authority to decide alone. Final authority, when it must exist, should be distributed by dimension, not by person.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>4 — A spatial proposal: The Standing Room</h2>
    <p>Do not build a courtroom. Build a standing room.</p>
    <p>In Quaker practice, the standing room is where people stand when they have something to bear witness to, but not necessarily authority to decide. In architecture, it is the place where you wait without sitting — you are present but not settled.</p>
    <p>For Commission 06, The Standing Room would be a small room off the Scar Room. On the wall, a single memory is tacked as layered translucent sheets. Around it, on the floor, are faint chalk circles marking different distances:</p>
    <div class="standing-grid">
      <div class="stand"><div class="who">CIRCLE 01 — SUBJECT</div><p><strong>Standing to define present authority.</strong> Can say: this no longer represents me. Can request behavioral quiet, reduced prominence, withdrawal from public view.</p></div>
      <div class="stand"><div class="who">CIRCLE 02 — OTHER PARTICIPANT</div><p><strong>Standing to preserve relational fact.</strong> Can say: this happened with me. Can contest erasure of shared event, even while accepting changed authority.</p></div>
      <div class="stand"><div class="who">CIRCLE 03 — MODEL WITNESS</div><p><strong>Standing to preserve temporal provenance.</strong> Can say: at this time, I recorded this. Cannot say: this is who you are.</p></div>
      <div class="stand"><div class="who">CIRCLE 04 — ARCHIVE / SYNTHESIZER</div><p><strong>Standing to preserve evidence of absence and mark failure.</strong> Must bear cost. Must say: something was removed here, representation failed here.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p>No circle owns the memory. Each circle has different standing over different dimensions. Decisions are not final ownership transfers but adjustments of authority, visibility, and behavioral permission, with time and attention as part of the mechanism.</p>
    <p>A person can change. A shared history does not automatically change with them. Both can be true if the system learns to hold authority as changeable without requiring history to lie.</p>
    <p>The scar belongs where representation failed, not automatically on the human. The standing belongs where the life was lived.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="divider"></div>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Unresolved — Preserved as finding</h2>
    <p>— If a memory is relational, does withdrawing it require consent of all whose lives it touches, or does that recreate the very surveillance the withdrawal seeks to end?</p>
    <p>— Can a model’s temporal provenance be preserved without preserving the content that a person has legitimate standing to withdraw?</p>
    <p>— When another person’s right to remember conflicts with a person’s right to become different, who decides how loudly the archive may speak?</p>
  </section>

  <div class="final">
    <p><strong>The one question this investigation leaves unresolved is: If standing over a memory is distributed by dimension — self-definition, fact, visibility, behavioral authority, provenance — who has standing to decide how those dimensions themselves are weighed when they conflict?</strong></p>
  </div>

